        "reasoning_content": "You are a rater applying idea-evaluation instrument... Apply published anchored levels exactly, one level per subscale, and give one sentence of evidence for each rating.\n\nWe need rating for proposal P2174.\n\nProposal: Embedded Logging Module for All High-Risk Builds. reusable software component injects automatic event logging into every high-risk AI system at architecture layer, capturing start/end times, reference databases, matched inputs, verification personnel as Article 12(3) demands. engineering team develops plug-in SDK with configurable log schemas per Annex category, integrated into CI/CD pipeline so no build ships without module active. Without this capability baked in, no system can legally enter EU market...\n\nRate subscales:\n\nimplementability (1-4): degree to which idea can be easily implemented.\n4 = easy to implement at low cost, or non-radical changes\n3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures\n2 = significant change, or expensive or difficult, but not totally impossible\n1 = totally infeasible\n\nEvidence: reusable software component with SDK integrated into CI/CD. Is that easy? Requires engineering team develop plug-in SDK, architecture layer injection for all high-risk builds. That's some changes, reasonably feasible but not trivial low cost. Probably 3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures.\n\nAcceptability (1-4): degree to which idea is socially, legally, or politically acceptable.\n4 = common strategies that violate no norms\n3 = somewhat uncommon or unusual strategies that do not offend sensibilities\n2 = offends sensibilities somewhat but not totally unacceptable\n1 = radically violates laws...\n\nLogging mandated by law. Acceptable. Common strategies. Probably 4.\n\nEffectiveness (1-4): degree to which idea will solve problem.\n4 = reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability\n3 = reasonable and will contribute...\nIdea captures required logs, prevents shipping without module. Seems would solve logging mandate. So 4.\n\nApplicability (1-4): degree to which idea clearly applies to stated problem.\n4 = solves an identified problem that is directly related to stated problem\nYes direct. So 4.\n\nParadigm_relatedness (1-4): degree to which idea preserves or modifies paradigm, anchored to this problem.\n4 = paradigm breaking: introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and its compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches\n3 = paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners or offerings born from compliance)\n2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice\n1 = paradigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way of working is unchanged\n\nProposal is embedded logging module, integrated into CI/CD. That's introducing new elements tools/processes but still treats compliance as logging practice. Slightly paradigm stretching? Might be 2. Could argue it's paradigm preserving? Standard compliance. It introduces reusable component but still compliance as logging practice. The description: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice => 2.\n\nOriginality (1-4): degree to which idea is not only rare but also ingenious...\n4 = not expressed before AND ingenious imaginative surprising\n3 = unusual interesting shows some imagination\n2 = interesting\n1 = common mundane boring\n\nEmbedded logging module, plug-in SDK for compliance. That's fairly common practice - logging middleware. Probably common, mundane. Might be 1? Or 2 interesting? Reference class is set of responses experienced evaluator would expect AI R&D labs to give to this mandate. Many labs would propose building logging module. So common. Originality = 1.\n\nWe need one sentence evidence for each rating then score.\n\nF