cability (1-4): degree to which idea clearly applies to stated problem.\n4 = solves identified problem directly related\n3 = solves implied problem related\n2 = may have some benefit within special situation and somehow relates\n1 = intervention not stated...\n\nProposal is about documentation workflow tied to sprints. Does it apply to logging mandate? Article 12 is record-keeping logs. Documentation is separate but Recital 71 links them. Might be directly related? The mandate includes keeping technical documentation up to date. Actually Article 12 is record-keeping logs, but Recital says technical documentation should be kept up to date. The proposal targets documentation updates. Could be applicability 3 = solves implied problem related to stated problem.\n\nMaybe applicability = 3. It solves implied problem of traceability/documentation which relates to logging mandate.\n\nLet's decide scores:\nacceptability: evidence sentence then 4\nimplementability: 4? Or 3. I'll choose 4 easy.\nparadigm_relatedness: 2\noriginality: 2\neffectiveness: 3? Let's reconsider: Problem is meet logging mandate. Proposal does not address automatic recording of events/logs. It addresses documentation. So effectiveness maybe 2 = unreasonable or unlikely to solve problem (it probably will not work). Because it doesn't provide logs. The proposal mentions technical documentation updates, not logging capabilities per Article 12(1-3). So it probably will not solve logging mandate. Could be 2.\n\nApplicability: 3? It may have some benefit but somehow relates. Maybe 2 = may have some benefit within special situation and somehow relates to stated problem. Because it's documentation not logs. I'd say applicability 2.\n\nLet's choose final:\nacceptability 4\nimplementability 4 (or 3). Choose 3 to be safe: some changes reasonably feasible. Might be easy but requires cultural change. I'll go with 3.\n\nLet's finalize:\n\nacceptability_evidence: The protocol relies on internal process changes and sta