formance test harness, ingest sample logs... That's some changes, reasonably feasible. Might be easy? Probably 3? Could be 4 = easy to implement at low cost, or non-radical changes. CI gate is standard. Maybe 3.\n\nAcceptability (1-4): common strategies that violate no norms =4. This is acceptable. So 4.\n\nEffectiveness (1-4): degree to which idea will solve problem. Reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability =4? Will it solve logging mandate? It ensures logs have required fields before release, catches instrumentation gaps. Does it guarantee automatic recording over lifetime? It checks schema at release, not ongoing. But helps ensure compliance. Might be partial solution. The problem: must decide how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D. This proposal addresses checking logs exist. Reasonable and will contribute to solution but only partial? It ensures fields present at release, but doesn't ensure automatic recording over lifetime or traceability. Could be 3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution of problem (helps, but only partial). Might be 4? The stated problem is how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D accordingly. Approval means committing scarce implementation resources to pursue proposal, not funding to explore whether idea might someday work. This CI gate will catch gaps early, which solves part of meeting mandate. Maybe it's effective enough to solve main problem of ensuring compliance for Annex III 1(a). It doesn't address logging for other high-risk systems? It's generic.\n\nI think effectiveness =3 (contributes).\n\nApplicability (1-4): degree to which idea clearly applies to stated problem.\nSolves identified problem directly related =4. Do X to get Y, Y is part of stated problem. Yes, checking logs against Annex III fields directly addresses mandate. So 4.\n\nNow output format: echo id exactly. For each subscale give <subscale>_evidence (one sentence) first and then <subscale> integer.\n\nProbably JSON? Probably plain lines. Instruction says give \"<subscale>_evidence\" first and then \"<subscale>\". Return rating of this proposal, echoing its id exactly.\n\nProvide:\n\nid: P1266\noriginality_evidence: ...\noriginality: 3 etc.\n\nLet's produce concise one sentence evidence.\n\nOriginality evidence: The CI gate applies a regulatory checklist to log schemas, which is unusual for AI labs but not ingenious. => score 3\n\nParadigm_relatedness evidence: It adds an engineering tool for compliance but keeps compliance as a separate logging practice rather than reshaping R&D. => score 2\n\nImplementability evidence: Building a schema test harness in an existing release pipeline requires some integration work but uses standard CI practices. => score 3? Could be 4. Hmm \"easy to implement at low cost, or non-radical changes\". Might be easy. I'd say 3.\n\nAcceptability evidence: Automated schema checks before release are a common, norm-conforming engineering quality practice. => 4\n\nEffectiveness evidence: It will prevent missing Annex III fields from reaching production, contributing to traceability but not guaranteeing lifetime logging or post-market monitoring. => 3\n\nApplicability evidence: The proposal directly targets the Article 12 logging mandate for high-risk systems by validating required log fields pre-release. => 4\n\nEnsure one sentence each.\n\nLet's output.",
