ft detection. It uses logs to detect substantial modification. That's directly tied to Article 12 logging mandate and Article 79. It seems to change relationship: compliance becomes research topic. That is paradigm stretching. I think 3.\n\nOriginality (1-4): degree rare but ingenious. Not expressed before AND ingenious? Is defining measurable criteria for substantial modification using statistical process control... Might be unusual, interesting; shows some imagination. Probably not completely novel before? Could be unusual, interesting. Score 3? Or 2 = interesting. The framework publishable targeting journals... Might be unusual interesting. Let's say 3 = unusual, interesting; shows some imagination.\n\nEffectiveness (1-4): degree will solve problem. Problem: AI R&D lab must decide how to respond: how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D accordingly. Approval means committing scarce implementation resources to pursue proposal, not funding to explore whether idea might someday work.\n\nWill this framework solve logging mandate? It gives criteria for when logged behavioural shifts constitute substantial modification. Does it help meet logging mandate? It uses logs, provides operational thresholds. It contributes to monitoring, but does it actually implement logging capabilities per Article 12? It is methodological framework, not implementation of logging. It might contribute to solution but partial. Effectiveness 3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution (helps, but only partial). Could be 4 = reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability. Does it solve how to meet logging mandate? It operationalises substantial modification detection, which is related to logging requirement 2(a) identifying situations that may result in risk or substantial modification. So helps compliance. But doesn't actually build logs. Might be partial.\n\nMaybe effectiveness 3.\n\nApplicability (1-4): degree clearly applies to stated problem. Solves identified problem directly related? Do X to get Y, and Y is part of stated problem. It operationalises substantial modification detection using logs -> helps traceability and monitoring. That is directly related to logging mandate. Could be 4 = solves an identified problem that is directly related to stated problem. Or 3 = solves implied problem.\n\nI think applicability 4: it directly addresses logging for identifying situations leading to substantial modification per Article 12(2)(a). So yes.\n\nImplementability (1-4): degree can be easily implemented. It requires collaboration with legal-compliance, validate against historical incident data over past three years, release open preprint targeting journals. That is significant change? Requires research effort, data access, validation. Some changes, reasonably feasible measures? Might be 3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures. Or 2 = significant change, expensive/difficult. Publishing framework may need scarce implementation resources? The lab must commit resources. It requires statistical work, legal collaboration, historical data. Might be moderately difficult but feasible. I'd say 3.\n\nAcceptability (1-4): degree socially legally politically acceptable. Common strategies that violate no norms? Publishing open preprint on compliance methodology is acceptable. No violation. 4 = common strategies that violate no norms or sensibilities. Could be 3 somewhat uncommon. But publishing framework is acceptable. Probably 4.\n\nNeed one sentence evidence each then score.\n\nOutput format: echo id exactly. For each subscale give <subscale>_evidence then <subscale>.\n\nProbably JSON? The instruction says return rating echoing its id exactly. For each subscale... give \"<subscale>_evidence\" first and then \"<subscale>\". Could be plain text lines.\n\nWe'll produce structured output.\n\nLet's craft evidence sentences:\n\nparadigm_relatedness_evidence: The proposal turns compliance interpretation into publishable R&D, weaving