ogging mandate? It adds transparency but core logging still needed. Might contribute partially. Effectiveness 3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution (partial). Not fully solve logging creation but helps traceability and post-market monitoring. Maybe 3.\n\nApplicability: degree clearly applies to stated problem. Solves identified problem directly related? The problem is meet logging mandate. Dashboard is about making logs accessible to buyers, not necessarily internal record-keeping. It relates to Article 12 requirement for logging capabilities enabling recording... Might be downstream. It applies to compliance but more commercial. Probably 3 = solves an implied problem that is related (trust signal). Or 4 = solves identified problem directly related? Logging mandate requires technical ability to record events; dashboard does not create logs, just displays. So applicability maybe 3.\n\nParadigm_relatedness: degree preserves/modifies paradigm anchored to this problem.\nLevels:\n4 = paradigm breaking: introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and its compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches\n3 = paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners or offerings born from compliance)\n2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice\n1 = paradigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice\n\nProposal converts regulatory obligation into visible trust signal differentiating in procurement. That's new partners/offerings born from compliance. That changes relationship. So paradigm stretching = 3.\n\nOriginality: degree rare but ingenious...\n4 = not expressed before AND ingenious imaginative surprising\n3 = unusual interesting shows some imagination\n2 = interesting\n1 = common mundane\n\nClient-facing log transparency dashboard for EU buyers is unusual but maybe not super i