mmon strategies that violate no norms.\n\nEffectiveness: degree to which idea will solve problem.\n4 = reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability (if you could do it, it would solve main problem)\n3 = reasonable and will contribute...\nProposal directly addresses logging mandate automatic recording of events logs over lifetime, timestamps, inputs, reference DB, verification actions. Covers Article 12(1) and 12(3). Seems will solve. So 4.\n\nApplicability: degree to which idea clearly applies to stated problem.\n4 = solves identified problem that is directly related to stated problem\nYes direct. So 4.\n\nparadigm_relatedness: degree to which idea preserves or modifies paradigm anchored to this problem.\n4 = paradigm breaking: introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches\n3 = paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners or offerings born from compliance)\n2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice\n1 = paradigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way of working unchanged\n\nProposal introduces universal logging layer built into dev stack, default platform capability. Is that just tools? It still treats compliance as logging practice, introduces new elements but doesn't change relationship? Might be slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements tools/processes but still treats compliance as logging practice. Could be 2. Possibly 3? Logging woven into science? It says without disturbing research workflows. Maybe not change relationship. I'd say 2.\n\noriginality: degree to which idea is rare but ingenious.\n4 = not expressed before AND ingenious...\n3 = unusual, interesting; shows some imagination\n2 = interesting\n1 = common, mundane, boring\n\nEmbedding mandatory teleme