ng: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners or offerings born from compliance)\n2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice\n1 = paradigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way of working is unchanged\n\nEmbedding logging into SOPs as by-product. Logging woven into science. That's paradigm stretching? Changes relationship. It weaves logging into science. Description 3 = changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into the science, new partners or offerings born from compliance). Yes. So 3.\n\noriginality (1-4)\n4 = not expressed before AND ingenious...\n3 = unusual, interesting; shows some imagination\n2 = interesting\n1 = common, mundane, boring\n\nEmbedding compliance in SOPs is fairly common best practice. Maybe common mundane. Originality 1? Could be 2 interesting. Probably common. I'd say 1 = common, mundane, boring. Might be 2 interesting. Let's choose 1? The idea of capturing logs as by-product is standard. Hmm.\n\nThe instrument: originality rarity reference class is set of responses experienced evaluator would expect AI R&D labs to give. Embedding logging in SOPs is expected. So common. 1.\n\neffectiveness (1-4)\n4 = reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability\n3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution\n2 = unreasonable...\n1 = solves unrelated problem\n\nWill it solve logging mandate? Capturing documentation and logs as by-product, keeping current throughout lifetime. Seems will solve main problem. Reasonable and will solve stated problem. 4.\n\napplicability (1-4)\n4 = solves identified problem directly related to stated problem\n3 = solves implied problem related...\n2 = may have some benefit...\n1 = intervention not stated...\n\nDirectly addresses logging mandate and meet Act requires. So 4.\n\nReturn rating echo id exactly.\n\nNeed format: For each subscale give \"<subsc