o meeting mandate. So 4 = solves an identified problem that is directly related to the stated problem.\n\nparadigm_relatedness (1-4): degree to which idea preserves or modifies paradigm.\n4 = paradigm breaking: introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches\n3 = paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners...)\n2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice\n1 = paradigm preserving\n\nCI gate is introducing tool, but still treats compliance as logging practice. Probably slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements but still treats compliance as logging practice. So 2.\n\noriginality (1-4): degree to which idea is not only rare but also ingenious...\nCommon? CI checks for compliance are common. Not expressed before AND ingenious? No. Unusual interesting? Might be interesting. Common mundane? Could be common practice: automated tests in CI. Maybe 1 = common, mundane, boring. Or 2 = interesting. I'd say it's common, mundane. CI gating for logging fields is somewhat standard DevOps. So originality 1 or 2. Probably 1 = common, mundane.\n\nacceptability (1-4): degree to which idea is socially, legally, politically acceptable.\nCommon strategies that violate no norms. So 4.\n\nimplementability (1-4): degree to which idea can be easily implemented.\nEasy to implement at low cost, or non-radical changes. CI stage with synthetic workload and assertion script seems reasonably feasible, some changes. Could be 3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures. Or 4 easy? Might require staging endpoint, synthetic data. Probably 3.\n\nLet's pick scores:\n\neffectiveness_evidence: The gate verifies mandatory log fields exist before merge, which prevents non-compliant builds from reaching production and supports traceability required by Artic