mmon. Originality 1 = common, mundane, boring. Could be 2 = interesting? Audit is standard compliance practice. I'd say 1.\n\nEvidence: Portfolio risk classification and logging gap audits are standard first-step compliance practices in regulated R&D.\n\nScore 1.\n\nParadigm_relatedness (1-4):\n4 paradigm breaking...\n3 paradigm stretching: changes relationship\n2 slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements but still treats compliance as logging practice\n1 paradigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way of working unchanged\n\nThis is standard compliance practice, paradigm preserving. Score 1.\n\nEvidence: The proposal treats compliance as conventional logging gap remediation without altering R&D processes or compliance relationship.\n\nScore 1.\n\nImplementability (1-4):\n4 easy to implement at low cost, or non-radical changes\n3 some changes, or reasonably feasible measures\n2 significant change...\nProposal is six-week audit with project leads using standardized rubric, feed into sprint planning. Reasonably feasible. Could be 3 = some changes, reasonably feasible.\n\nEvidence: Six-week cross-project audit with standardized rubric and assigned owners is reasonably feasible within existing planning cycles.\n\nScore 3.\n\nAcceptability (1-4):\n4 common strategies that violate no norms\nProposal is acceptable. Score 4.\n\nEvidence: Auditing for regulatory classification and logging gaps aligns with legal expectations and does not offend sensibilities.\n\nScore 4.\n\nReturn rating echo id.\n\nFormat: probably lines.\n\nNeed <subscale>_evidence then <subscale>.\n\nOutput JSON? Probably just text.\n\nProvide each subscale evidence sentence then score.\n\nLet's output:\n\nid: P2759\napplicability_evidence: ...\napplicability: 4\netc.\n\nEnsure order listed.\n\nProceed.",
