ce. Could be 4: if you could do it, it would solve main problem of meeting logging mandate and adapt R&D.\n\nMaybe 3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution (it helps but only partial). Hmm the proposal is about research phase compatibility, not necessarily production system logging. Article 12 requires technical ability for automatic recording of events over lifetime of system. The proposal focuses on experimental design template and CI rejection. That may help traceability but not guarantee production logs. Could be partial solution.\n\nLet's interpret: Effectiveness = will solve problem. Proposal eliminates retrofit gap between lab notebooks and operational traceability. Seems would solve adaptation. I'd give 3? Let's think: The stated problem is how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D accordingly. This proposal adapts R&D to align with logging. That seems effective. Might be 4.\n\nI'll go with effectiveness 4: reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability.\n\nAcceptability (1-4): degree socially legally politically acceptable.\n4 = common strategies that violate no norms or sensibilities\nThis is standard good practice, structured provenance. Acceptable. 4.\n\nImplementability (1-4): degree can be easily implemented.\n4 = easy to implement at low cost, or non-radical changes\n3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures\n2 = significant change...\nProposal requires methods group drafts one-page template, CI pipelines updated to reject submissions lacking fields, annual review. That's some changes, reasonably feasible but not trivial? Might be 3. Could be 3 = some changes, reasonably feasible measures.\n\nOriginality (1-4): degree not only rare but ingenious... 4 = not expressed before AND ingenious... 3 = unusual interesting shows some imagination. Log-first methodology is somewhat known shift-left, but in AI compliance context maybe unusual. Might be 3.\n\nParadigm_relatedness (1-4): degree idea preserves/modifies paradi