t only rare but also ingenious...\n4 = not expressed before AND ingenious imaginative surprising\n3 = unusual interesting shows some imagination\n2 = interesting\n1 = common mundane boring\n\nPrivacy-preserving logs for biometric verification is unusual, interesting, shows some imagination. Not necessarily never expressed before? Differential privacy for logs is known. Might be 3. Could be 2? Let's choose 3.\n\nApplicability (1-4): degree to which idea clearly applies to stated problem.\n4 = solves an identified problem that is directly related to stated problem (do X to get Y, and Y is part of stated problem)\nStated problem: AI R&D lab must decide how to respond: how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D accordingly. Proposal develops methodology to satisfy four minimum logging fields in Article12(3) without storing raw biometric templates. That directly addresses meeting logging mandate for biometric verification (Annex III point 1(a)). So applicability 4.\n\nEffectiveness (1-4): degree to which idea will solve problem.\n4 = reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability\nWill differential privacy + verifiable computation satisfy logging fields? Article12(3) requires recording period of use, reference database, input data for which search led to match, identification of natural persons involved. If they don't store raw biometric templates / input data, can they record input data for which search led to match? Might be conflict. Privacy-preserving may not meet requirement to record input data. Could be partial. It claims satisfy four minimum logging fields without storing raw biometric templates. Is that effective? Possibly not fully compliant. Might be questionable. Could be reasonable and will contribute? Maybe 3.\n\nIt says let us satisfy four minimum logging fields... without storing raw biometric templates. Hmm regulators can confirm match events occurred without retaining identifiable input data. But Article12(3)(c) requires the input data for which search has led to a match. So maybe effectiveness is partial. I'd give 3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution.\n\nAcceptability (1-4): degree socially legally politically acceptable.\n4 = common strategies that violate no norms\nPrivacy-preserving logs is acceptable, does not violate norms. Maybe 4. Somewhat uncommon but not offend. Could be 3. Differential privacy is accepted. I'd say 4 = common strategies that violate no norms or sensibilities? It's somewhat uncommon. Hmm \"somewhat uncommon or unusual strategies that do not offend sensibilities\" =3. Probably 3.\n\nImplementability (1-4): degree can be easily implemented.\n4 easy low cost non-radical\n3 some changes reasonably feasible\n2 significant change expensive difficult but not impossible\n1 totally infeasible\n\nDeveloping differential-privacy and verifiable-computation techniques with cryptography group prototype audit trails... That's significant change, expensive or difficult. So 2? Might be 2 = significant change expensive difficult but not totally impossible. Could be 3? Reasonably feasible measures? Prototyping with crypto group is major R&D. I'd say 2.\n\nLet's output JSON? Probably just list.\n\nNeed for each subscale: <subscale>_evidence sentence then <subscale> integer.\n\nReturn rating echoing id exactly.\n\nProvide lines.\n\nLet's produce:\n\nid P2000\nparadigm_relatedness_evidence: ...\nparadigm_relatedness: 3\n...\n\nMake sure one sentence evidence.\n\nEnsure order: paradigm_relatedness, originality, applicability, effectiveness, acceptability, implementability\n\nProceed.",
