ce, new partners or offerings born from compliance)\n2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice\n1 = paradigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way of working is unchanged\n\nProposal is basically build log retention platform. Introduces new tools/processes but still treats compliance as logging practice. Slightly paradigm stretching? Could be paradigm preserving? It says governs where deployed-system logs live after release. That's standard compliance. Probably 1 = paradigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way unchanged. Or 2 slightly paradigm stretching introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice. The description says data-infrastructure project... This is new element. Might be 2. Hmm.\n\nThe anchor: 1 = standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way of working is unchanged. Actually building platform does change way of working? Maybe slightly. I'd lean 2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice.\n\nOriginality: degree rare but ingenious...\n4 = not expressed before AND ingenious...\n3 = unusual interesting shows some imagination\n2 = interesting\n1 = common mundane boring\n\nLifetime log retention platform is common mundane. Probably 1. Could be 2 interesting? I think common. 1.\n\nApplicability: degree clearly applies to stated problem.\n4 = solves identified problem directly related to stated problem (do X to get Y, and Y is part of stated problem)\n3 = solves implied problem...\n2 = may have some benefit within special situation...\n1 = intervention not stated...\n\nProposal stores logs for lifetime, with access controls matching Annex III. Directly addresses logging mandate. So 4.\n\nEffectiveness: degree will solve problem.\n4 = reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability (if you could do it, it would solve main problem)\n3 = reasonable and will contribute...\n2 = unreasonable...\n1 = solves unrelated...\n\nWill it solve? It provides retention and retrieval, which enables traceability and post-market monitoring. Seems will solve logging mandate. So 4? Might be partial because doesn't address automatic recording? Article 12 requires technically allow automatic recording. This platform is retention/retrieval after release, not ensuring logs are generated. Recital says logs should be recorded automatically. The proposal governs where logs live after release; may assume logs already captured. Could be partial. Hmm.\n\nThe problem: must decide how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D accordingly. Proposal is about retention/retrieval platform. That addresses record-keeping but not automatic recording capability? It says stores, indexes, makes retrievable operational logs across lifetime, with access controls matching Annex III identity-recording obligations. Governs where deployed-system logs live after release. Recital 71 requires documentation remain current; without purpose-built retrieval we cannot demonstrate traceability...\n\nEffectiveness: reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability? If you could do it, would it solve main problem of meeting logging mandate? Possibly not full automatic recording, but retention/retrieval is part. Article 12(1) requires technically allow automatic recording. Platform doesn't create logging capability, just stores. Might be partial.\n\nThe text: \"This governs where deployed-system logs live after release.\" So assumes logs exist. Could be effectiveness 3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution (partial). I'd pick 3.\n\nNow format output: echo id exactly. For each subscale in order listed, give \"<subscale>_evidence\" (one sentence) first and then \"<subscale>\" integer.\n\nSo lines:\n\nP1565\n\nacceptability_evidence: ...\naccepta