asonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability (if you could do it, it would solve main problem). Will producing logging spec gate ensure meet logging mandate? It helps but does not guarantee logs are actually implemented correctly in production, only spec. Might contribute partial solution. Could be 3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution of problem (it helps, but only partial). The problem is decide how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D. Spec gate addresses design phase. Probably contributes but not full solve? Might still need implementation. I think effectiveness 3.\n\nApplicability: degree idea clearly applies to stated problem. 4 = solves identified problem that is directly related to stated problem (do X to get Y, and Y is part of stated problem). Do spec gate to meet logging mandate. Yes directly related. Score 4.\n\nParadigm_relatedness: degree preserves/modifies paradigm. Anchored: 4 = paradigm breaking: introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches. 3 = paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners...). 2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements but still treats compliance as logging practice. 1 = paradigm preserving.\n\nProposal shifts compliance left into design phase, spec written by developers not lawyers. That's introducing new elements but still treats compliance as logging practice. Might be slightly paradigm stretching? Logging woven into science? Could be paradigm stretching: changes relationship - compliance becomes engineering deliverable. I think 2? Slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice. Yes spec is process. So 2.\n\nOriginality: degree rare but ingenious. 4 = not expressed before AND ingenious... 3 = unusual interesting shows some imagination. 2 = interesting. 1 = common mundane boring.\n\nLogging spec as prototyping gate is fairly common \"shift left\" compliance. Might be common mundane. Could be 2 = interesting? Hmm many labs do design review gates. Maybe common. I'd say 1 = common, mundane, boring. Or 2 interesting. The idea of requiring logging architecture spec before implementation is fairly standard for regulated software. Probably common. Let's give 2? Safer: common strategy. I think originality 2? Actually \"shift left compliance\" is not rare but not totally mundane? Might be 2 = interesting.\n\nThe instrument says reference class is set of responses experienced evaluator would expect AI R&D labs to give to this mandate. Expecting spec gate is fairly expected. So originality low. Maybe 1 = common, mundane, boring. I'd choose 1.\n\nLet's decide scores:\nacceptability_evidence: ... score 4\nimplementability_evidence: ... score 3\neffectiveness_evidence: ... score 3\napplicability_evidence: ... score 4\nparadigm_relatedness_evidence: ... score 2\noriginality_evidence: ... score 1? Could be 2.\n\nCheck definitions: originality 1 = common, mundane, boring. That fits shift left gate. So 1.\n\nProvide output with fields.\n\nReturn rating echoing id exactly.\n\nProbably JSON format.\n\nProvide each subscale_evidence sentence then subscale integer.\n\nLet's produce.\n\n",
