 reasonable and will contribute to solution of problem (it helps, but only partial). Might be 4? If you could do it, would solve main problem? No, need technical logging first. So effectiveness 3.\n\nNovelty:\noriginality (1-4): degree to which idea is not only rare but ingenious...\nCommon, mundane, boring? Internal audit trail, ownership, dashboard, escalation path is common compliance practice. So originality 1 = common, mundane, boring.\n\nparadigm_relatedness (1-4): degree to which idea preserves or modifies paradigm.\nParadigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way of working is unchanged. This is standard governance. So 1.\n\nWorkability:\nacceptability (1-4): degree to which idea is socially, legally, politically acceptable.\nCommon strategies that violate no norms. Yes. So 4.\n\nimplementability (1-4): degree to which idea can be easily implemented.\nSome changes, or reasonably feasible measures? Assigning ownership, dashboard, quarterly audits. Might be some changes, reasonably feasible. Could be 3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures. Not easy low cost? Requires engineer per product line, dashboard. Might be moderate. I'd say 3.\n\nCheck anchors: 4 = easy to implement at low cost, or non-radical changes. 3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures. This seems more than non-radical. So 3.\n\nProvide output with subscale_evidence and subscale.\n\nFormat: For each subscale in order listed: FIRST write one sentence of evidence for that subscale (your reasoning before the number). THEN give its score as integer level.\n\nProbably need JSON? The instruction says return rating echoing its id exactly. For each subscale... give \"<subscale>_evidence\" first and then \"<subscale>\"...\n\nProbably output lines.\n\nLet's produce:\n\nid: P2881\n\napplicability_evidence: ... \napplicability: 4\netc.\n\nEnsure one sentence evidence each.\n\nMake sure sentence ends with period.\n\nProceed.",
