al implementation? The idea is methodological, likely will contribute to solution but maybe not guarantee full compliance? I'd say 3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution of problem (it helps but only partial). However description says elevates rigor making operational transparency design constraint from day one, yielding more reproducible... Could be full solution.\n\nAnchored: 4 = reasonable and will solve the stated problem without regard for workability (if you could do it, it would solve main problem). I think yes. If you could implement methodology, you'd meet logging mandate. So 4.\n\nparadigm_relatedness: degree to which idea preserves or modifies paradigm. Levels: 4 paradigm breaking: introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches. 3 paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners...). 2 slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements but still treats compliance as logging practice. 1 preserving.\n\nProposal integrates logging into experimental design, makes operational transparency design constraint. That changes relationship between R&D and compliance obligations – logging woven into science. That's paradigm stretching? Could be 3 = changes that relationship. Maybe also 4? Introduces new elements AND changes relationship... It does introduce event-mapping document, lightweight hooks, quarterly audits. Changes relationship. Does it address larger problem of what lab researches? Not really. So 3.\n\noriginality: degree to which idea is not only rare but ingenious. Reference class set of responses experienced evaluator would expect AI R&D labs to give to mandate. Integrating compliance early is somewhat unusual but not extremely rare? Might be interesting. Levels: 4 = not expressed before AND ingenious imaginative surprising. 3 = unusual, interesting; shows some imagination. 2 = interesting. 1 = common