stem's event schema. That's some changes, reasonably feasible measures? Might require building domain language, model checker. Could be significant change. Probably 3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures. Not easy low cost. I'd say 3.\n\nAcceptability: degree to which idea is socially legally politically acceptable. Common strategies that violate no norms. It's internal compliance tool. So 4 = common strategies that violate no norms.\n\nParadigm_relatedness: degree to which idea preserves or modifies paradigm anchored to this problem.\nOptions:\n4 = paradigm breaking: introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and its compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches\n3 = paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners or offerings born from compliance)\n2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice\n1 = paradigm preserving\n\nThis is spec-as-code validation pipeline shifting compliance verification left. Introduces new elements tools/processes but still treats compliance as logging practice. Might be slightly paradigm stretching? It weaves logging into development loop. Could be 2 or 3. The description: changes that relationship (logging woven into science). Might be 3? \"shifts compliance verification left into the development loop\" suggests logging woven into science. That is paradigm stretching per definition: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into the science, new partners...). I'd say 3.\n\nOriginality: degree to which idea is not only rare but also ingenious...\nIs spec-as-code validation pipeline for AI Act logging common? Spec-as-code is known; model checking logs is somewhat unusual. Might be unusual interesting shows some imagination = 3. Not completely novel. Could be 3.\n\nAlternatively originality 2 = interesting. I'd lean 3.\n\nProvide output for