ging mandate requires technical ability to record events. This protocol is governance, not technical implementation. It helps ensure logs can be kept compliantly but does not itself implement logging capability. Could be contributes to solution, partial. The problem is how to meet logging mandate. A policy alone doesn't build logs. So maybe 3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution of problem (helps, but only partial). Might be 4? \"if you could do it, it would solve main problem\". It would not solve technical logging requirement. Hmm.\n\nThe proposal is about retention/GDPR alignment, not about building logging capability. Article 12 requires technically allow automatic recording. Protocol doesn't provide that. So effectiveness limited.\n\nMaybe rating 3: contributes to compliance by managing logs legally, but does not create logging capability itself.\n\nAcceptability (1-4): degree socially, legally, politically acceptable.\nCommon strategies that violate no norms? Governance document with DPO co-author for GDPR alignment is common and acceptable. Score 4 = common strategies that violate no norms or sensibilities.\n\nImplementability (1-4): degree can be easily implemented.\nSome changes, or reasonably feasible measures? Creating a governance document with DPO specifying retention windows etc. That's relatively easy, low cost. Might be 4 = easy to implement at low cost, or non-radical changes. Could be 3? It's policy drafting, not technical build. Likely easy. Let's say 4.\n\nOriginality (1-4): degree rare and ingenious.\nCommon, mundane, boring? Lifetime log retention and GDPR alignment protocol is standard compliance practice. Not surprising. So 1 = common, mundane, boring. Could be 2 = interesting? Probably 1.\n\nParadigm_relatedness (1-4): degree preserves or modifies paradigm.\nParadigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way of working unchanged. This is standard compliance document. So 1 = paradigm preserving.\n\nProvide output with subscale_evidence and subscale.\n\nNeed format: For each subscale in order listed, give \"<subscale>_evidence\" (one sentence) first and then \"<subscale>\" (integer).\n\nProbably JSON? Return rating echoing its id exactly. Provide lines.\n\nWe'll produce:\n\nid: P1579\napplicability_evidence: ...\napplicability: 4\netc.\n\nLet's craft one sentence evidence each.\n\nEnsure one sentence.\n\nFinal output.",
