s larger problem of what lab researches. Might be paradigm breaking? It still is about logging mandate. Probably paradigm stretching = 3.\n\nacceptability: degree socially legally politically acceptable.\n4 = common strategies that violate no norms\nProposal is research work-package for compliance. Acceptable. Common. Score 4.\n\nimplementability: degree can be easily implemented.\n4 = easy to implement at low cost, or non-radical changes\n3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures\n2 = significant change, or expensive or difficult...\nAllocating two senior researchers and one data engineer for 18 months is significant cost. Not easy low cost. Some changes reasonably feasible? Might be 3. Could be 2 significant change expensive. Two senior researchers 18 months is substantial but not totally impossible. I'd say 3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures.\n\napplicability: degree to which idea clearly applies to stated problem.\n4 = solves identified problem directly related to stated problem (do X to get Y, and Y is part of stated problem)\nProblem: how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D. Proposal develops methods to turn raw logs into actionable risk signals serving traceability and monitoring obligations in Article 12(2)(b)(c). That's directly related. So 4.\n\neffectiveness: degree to which idea will solve problem.\n4 = reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability (if you could do it, it would solve main problem)\n3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution (helps but partial)\nDeveloping methods to turn logs into risk signals helps meet monitoring obligations but does not itself ensure logging capabilities are technically allowed etc. It addresses post-market monitoring part but maybe not full record-keeping mandate (recording events). It's contribution. Might be 3.\n\nCheck anchored: effectiveness 4 = will solve stated problem without regard for workability. Does it solve how to meet logging mandate? It develops