nEvidence: Automatic lifecycle logging from first commit directly satisfies Article 12's mandatory event logging and traceability requirements.\n\nApplicability (1-4): degree to which idea clearly applies to stated problem.\n4 = solves identified problem directly related...\nYes directly. So 4.\n\nEvidence: The proposal targets meeting the EU AI Act logging mandate for high-risk AI systems in R&D.\n\nNovelty paradigm_relatedness (1-4):\n4 = paradigm breaking: introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches\n3 = paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners...)\n2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice\n1 = paradigm preserving: standard compliance...\n\nEmbedding logging into prototype harness is introducing tools but still treats compliance as logging practice. Slightly paradigm stretching? It weaves logging into development process. Could be 2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements but still treats compliance as logging practice. Or 3 = paradigm stretching changes relationship (logging woven into science). The description says embed into prototype harness, wire into CI so un-logged builds fail. That is weaving logging into science? Might be 2.\n\nI think 2 is appropriate: introduces middleware SDK but compliance remains logging practice.\n\nOriginality (1-4):\n4 = not expressed before AND ingenious...\n3 = unusual interesting shows some imagination\n2 = interesting\n1 = common mundane boring\n\nEmbedding logging middleware is common practice. Probably 1 = common, mundane, boring. Or 2 = interesting. Hmm many labs do SDK for logging. Might be common. I'd give 1.\n\nEvidence: Shared logging middleware and CI gating are standard software engineering practices for compliance.\n\nReturn rating echo id.\n\nFormat: need