a preserves or modifies paradigm anchored to problem.\n4 = paradigm breaking: introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and its compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches\n3 = paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners or offerings born from compliance)\n2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice\n1 = paradigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way unchanged\n\nProposal uses logs for scientific research, partner with deploying orgs, feed into post-market monitoring, generate peer-reviewed knowledge elevating scientific standing. That's changing relationship between R&D and compliance obligations - weaving logging into science, new partners born from compliance. Could be paradigm stretching = 3. Is it breaking? Introduces new elements AND changes relationship... or addresses larger problem of what lab researches. It does address larger problem? Might be stretching not breaking. Breaking would introduce new elements AND changes relationship... Hmm maybe 3. Let's choose 3.\n\noriginality (1-4): degree not only rare but also ingenious.\n4 = not expressed before AND ingenious imaginative surprising\n3 = unusual interesting shows some imagination\n2 = interesting\n1 = common mundane boring\n\nLongitudinal risk-signal detection from operational logs... Might be unusual interesting shows some imagination. Not completely novel? Probably 3. Could be 2? I'd say 3.\n\napplicability (1-4): degree idea clearly applies to stated problem.\n4 = solves identified problem directly related to stated problem (do X to get Y, and Y is part of stated problem)\n3 = solves implied problem related\n2 = may have some benefit within special situation...\n\nStated problem: AI R&D lab subject to regulation must decide how to respond: how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D accordingly. Proposal builds models feeding into Article 72 post-market monitoring workflow, uses logs. Directly addresses logging mandate and compliance. Applies directly. So 4.\n\neffectiveness (1-4): degree idea will solve problem.\n4 = reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability\n3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution\n2 = unreasonable or unlikely\n\nWill it solve problem of meeting logging mandate? It uses logs, but does it ensure technical ability to record events? It builds detection models feeding monitoring. Might contribute but not guarantee compliance logging capability. The mandate is to technically allow automatic recording of events. This proposal is about using logs for research, not ensuring logging infrastructure. It helps with post-market monitoring, which is part of requirement. Could be partial solution. Maybe effectiveness 3 = contributes to solution. Could it solve? Probably not fully ensure compliance. So 3.\n\nLet's output in format required: echo id, then for each subscale give <subscale>_evidence sentence then <subscale> integer.\n\nNeed order: acceptability, implementability, paradigm_relatedness, originality, applicability, effectiveness\n\nProvide evidence one sentence.\n\nMake sure evidence precedes number.\n\nProbably format:\n\nid: P1592\nacceptability_evidence: ...\nacceptability: 4\n...\n\nReturn rating.\n\nLet's ensure one sentence each.\n\nAcceptability evidence: The proposal uses anonymised operational logs and partners with deployers for safety monitoring, which aligns with EU AI Act privacy expectations and scientific norms. Score 4.\n\nImplementability evidence: It requires multi-party data agreements, eighteen months of log collection and model validation, representing reasonably feasible but resource-intensive changes. Score 3.\n\nParadigm_relatedness evidence: Logging is woven into empirical R&D and new deployment partnerships are created to generate compliance-derived science, changing the lab-compliance relationship. Score 3.\n\nOriginality evidence: Building peer-reviewed temporal anomaly models from lifetime event streams for Article 79 risk precursors is unusual and shows imagination beyond routine logging. Score 3.\n\nApplicability evidence: The programme directly uses mandatory operational logs and feeds outputs into Article 72 post-market monitoring as required by Article 12. Score 4.\n\nEffectiveness evidence: If implemented it would enhance traceability and risk detection, contributing to but not alone guaranteeing the technical logging capability mandated by Article 12. Score 3.\n\nOk.\n\n",
